steps.body:
A media query asks how wide the window is. That's the wrong question for a component, a piece of a page you reuse in different places, like a product card. The same card might sit in a narrow sidebar on one page and in a wide main column on another, on the same laptop screen. The window is the same width in both places, so a media query can't tell them apart. A **container query** asks how wide the card's box is instead.

## Making a container

First, mark the box the component lives in as a **container**, a box whose size its children are allowed to ask about:

```css
.card-slot {
  container-type: inline-size;
}
```

`inline-size` means children may ask about its width. (A container can also be `size`, to ask about the height too, but then its height can't come from its content, so it's rarely what you want.)

Then, anywhere inside it, a `@container` rule works like a media query, measured against that box:

```css
@container (width >= 28rem) {
  .card {
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
  }
}
```

When the card's container is at least 28rem wide, the card puts its picture beside its text. In a narrow container, the rule doesn't apply and the card stays stacked, wherever it is and however wide the window.

```diagram responsive-css-container-card
One card, two slots. In the narrow sidebar it stacks, and in the wide main column it goes side by side.
```

In the diagram, the same card is in two containers on one page. The sidebar is 18rem wide, so the query is false and the picture sits on top. The main column is 40rem wide, so the query is true and the picture moves to the left. Neither card knows where it is, and neither needs a special class for its place.

## Naming containers

A `@container` rule looks for the nearest container above the element it styles. When containers are nested, a name says which one you mean:

```css
.sidebar {
  container: sidebar / inline-size;
}

@container sidebar (width < 20rem) {
  .card h2 {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
}
```

`container` is a short way to write `container-name` and `container-type` together, with the name before the slash.

## Units that follow the container

Container queries come with their own units. `cqi` is a hundredth of the container's width, the way `vw` is a hundredth of the window's. With `font-size: clamp(1rem, 4cqi, 1.5rem)`, a card's title grows with its slot, from the `clamp()` of the previous part.

## Two rules to know

- **An element can't query itself.** A `@container` rule styles the children of a container, never the container, since changing the container's own size could change the answer to the query. That's why the examples put the container on a wrapper around the card, or make the card the container and style what's inside it.
- **A container can't be sized by its content.** Its width has to come from outside, from the box it's in. A block in normal flow is fine, since it fills its parent. A container that would shrink to fit its content, like a flex item with no `flex` size, has no content size to shrink to, so it collapses to nothing. Give such containers a width or `flex: 1`.

## Media queries or container queries?

Use media queries for the page's own layout: how many columns the page has, whether the navigation collapses, how big the page margins are. Use container queries for components that get reused in different places. Most modern sites use both, and container queries have worked in every current browser since 2023.

People set preferences on their own computer and phone: dark mode, less motion, more contrast. A page that ignores them feels wrong, and for some people it's unusable. A white page in a dark room is glaring, and an animation that swoops across the screen can make some people dizzy or sick. CSS can read every one of those settings.

## Tokens

A theme starts with design tokens, the named values for a site's colors, sizes, and spacing that you met in the lesson on the cascade, kept in custom properties on `:root`, the page's top element:

```css
:root {
  --color-bg: #ffffff;
  --color-text: #1b1b1f;
  --color-accent: #0b57d0;
}

body {
  background: var(--color-bg);
  color: var(--color-text);
}
```

Every other rule uses the tokens instead of writing colors directly. To change the theme, you change the tokens, and the whole site follows.

## Dark mode

`prefers-color-scheme` is a media query that's `dark` when someone has set their device to dark mode:

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-bg: #121216;
    --color-text: #e6e6ea;
    --color-accent: #8ab4f8;
  }
}
```

Only the tokens change, which is the payoff for using them. A dark theme isn't just inverted colors: use a dark gray rather than pure black, slightly softer text, and lighter accent colors, which stay readable on dark backgrounds.

Then tell the browser your page supports both, with one more line on `:root`:

```css
:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;
}
```

That's the **color-scheme** property. It tells the browser to draw its own parts of the page, like form fields, checkboxes, and scrollbars, in the matching scheme. Without it, a dark page gets glaring white text fields.

## light-dark()

`light-dark()` takes two colors and uses the first in a light scheme and the second in a dark one, so a token can hold both in one line, with no media query:

```css
:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;
  --color-bg: light-dark(#ffffff, #121216);
  --color-text: light-dark(#1b1b1f, #e6e6ea);
}
```

It follows `color-scheme`, so it only works when that's set, and it works in every current browser since 2024.

## A theme switch

Many sites let people pick a theme themselves, overriding the device's setting. The usual way is an attribute on the `html` element, like `data-theme="dark"`, which CSS can select:

```css
:root[data-theme="light"] {
  color-scheme: light;
}

:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
}
```

With `light-dark()` tokens, that's the whole theme switch in CSS: changing `color-scheme` flips every `light-dark()` color on the page. A few lines of JavaScript set the attribute when someone clicks the switch, and remember the choice for their next visit. The JavaScript lessons build exactly that.

## Less motion

`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is true when someone has turned on Reduce motion in their device's settings, often because movement on screen makes them dizzy or sick. Respect it by keeping big movements, like slides, zooms, and parallax scrolling, to people who haven't asked for less. The last part of this lesson shows how.

## Forced colors

Windows has **contrast themes**, which replace every color on every page with a small set the person chose, often light text on black, so they can read at all. CSS sees that as `forced-colors: active`. Your background colors are replaced and your shadows are removed, so anything that only shows by its background color, like a button with no border, disappears. Give buttons and inputs a border, even a transparent one: in forced colors, borders are drawn in the person's colors and become visible. Inside a `forced-colors` media query, you can use **system colors** like `CanvasText` and `ButtonText`, which name the person's chosen colors. Don't fight the mode: the person picked those colors on purpose.

A last setting, `prefers-contrast: more`, is true when someone asks for stronger contrast, a good time to darken gray text and thicken borders.

examples.code:
:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;
  --color-bg: light-dark(#ffffff, #121216);
  --color-surface: light-dark(#f4f4f6, #1e1e24);
  --color-text: light-dark(#1b1b1f, #e6e6ea);
  --color-muted: light-dark(#5f6368, #a0a0aa);
  --color-accent: light-dark(#0b57d0, #8ab4f8);
}

:root[data-theme="light"] {
  color-scheme: light;
}

:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
}

body {
  background: var(--color-bg);
  color: var(--color-text);
}

a {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}

.button {
  background: var(--color-accent);
  color: white;
  border: 2px solid transparent;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

@media (forced-colors: active) {
  .button:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid Highlight;
  }
}

steps.body:
Fonts are files, and a font that isn't on the reader's computer has to download before any text can show in it. A slow font can leave a page blank for seconds, or make the text jump when it finally arrives. Both are fixable, and this part shows how, along with the choice that skips the download entirely.

## Declaring a font

`@font-face` gives a font file a name you can use in `font-family`:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter";
  src: url("/fonts/inter.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: 100 900;
  font-display: swap;
}

body {
  font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

**WOFF2**, short for Web Open Font Format 2, is the format to use: a font squeezed small for the web, which every current browser reads. The `font-family` list on `body` is a **fallback stack**: the browser uses the first font it has, so it shows `system-ui` or any `sans-serif` font until Inter arrives, or if it never does.

A font in `@font-face` only downloads when some text on the page uses it, which is good: a font no text uses costs nothing.

## Invisible text, or text that changes

While a font downloads, the browser has two choices, and both have names:

- It can hide the text until the font arrives. That's a **FOIT**, a flash of invisible text: the page has its layout, but the words are blank.
- It can show the text in a fallback font, then swap in the real one. That's a **FOUT**, a flash of unstyled text: readable at once, but the text changes shape when the swap happens, and often moves things around.

`font-display` picks between them:

| Value | While the font downloads |
|---|---|
| `swap` | shows the fallback at once, and swaps whenever the font arrives |
| `fallback` | hides the text for a moment, then shows the fallback, and only swaps if the font arrives within about 3 seconds |
| `optional` | hides the text for a moment, and uses the font only if it's almost instant, otherwise keeps the fallback for this visit |
| `block` | hides the text for up to 3 seconds |

`swap` is the usual choice for text people need to read. `optional` suits a site where any movement is worse than the wrong font, and the font is usually cached by the second page anyway.

## Getting the font sooner

A browser only discovers it needs a font after it has the HTML and the CSS, and has worked out which text uses it. For the one or two fonts every page uses, a **preload** link in the head starts the download right away:

```html
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/inter.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
```

`crossorigin` looks odd for a file on your own site, but fonts are always fetched under the browser's rules for other origins, and without it the preloaded copy goes unused and the font downloads twice. Preload only what's used at the top of every page: everything you preload competes with everything else for the start of the connection.

Host font files on your own site when you can. A font from another company's server costs a DNS lookup and a new connection first, from the How the Web Works lessons, before the font itself can start.

## System fonts: no download at all

The fastest font is one the device already has. `system-ui` names the font the device uses for its own menus and buttons: San Francisco on Apple devices, Segoe UI on Windows, Roboto on Android. Many sites, including big ones, use it for everything. It looks at home on every device and never delays the text.

## Variable fonts

A font used to come as one file per weight: regular, bold, light, and so on, each another download. A **variable font** holds every weight in one file. The `font-weight: 100 900` in the `@font-face` above says this one file covers every weight from 100 to 900, so `font-weight: 650` works too. One file in place of four usually means fewer bytes and fewer requests.

## Stopping the jump

When the real font swaps in, its letters are usually a different width from the fallback's, so lines rewrap and everything below moves. That jump is the layout shift from the lesson on how a browser draws a page, and it's annoying when you're halfway through reading, or about to tap a button. `size-adjust` makes the fallback font closer in size to the real one, so the swap barely moves anything:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter fallback";
  src: local("Arial");
  size-adjust: 107%;
}

body {
  font-family: "Inter", "Inter fallback", sans-serif;
}
```

`local("Arial")` uses Arial from the reader's computer, so nothing downloads, and `size-adjust` scales it up to take about as much room as Inter. The right percentage depends on the two fonts, and font tools can measure it for you.

