  font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
}

steps.body:
Motion helps when it explains something: a menu that slides out of the button that opened it, a card that lifts a little when you point at it, a new message that eases in instead of popping up. Done badly, it makes a page stutter or makes some people feel sick. CSS has two tools for it, and choosing what to animate matters as much as how.

## Transitions

A **transition** animates a change from one value to another, so instead of jumping, the change takes a moment:

```css
.button {
  background-color: #0b57d0;
  transition: background-color 150ms ease-out, transform 150ms ease-out;
}

.button:hover {
  background-color: #0842a0;
  transform: translateY(-2px);
}
```

`transition` lists what to animate, how long it takes, and its **easing**, the way it speeds up or slows down. `ease-out` starts fast and slows to a stop, which feels natural for things reacting to you. `150ms` is 150 milliseconds. Small changes like hover effects feel best between about 100 and 300 milliseconds: faster looks like a jump, and slower feels like waiting. The transition runs in both directions, so the button eases back when the pointer leaves.

## Keyframe animations

A transition needs something to change, like a hover. An **animation** runs by itself, from steps you describe in `@keyframes`:

```css
@keyframes slide-in {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(1rem);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}

.toast {
  animation: slide-in 200ms ease-out;
}
```

When a `.toast` appears on the page, it fades in while sliding up from 1rem below. `animation` can also repeat, with a count or `infinite`, which suits a loading spinner.

## Animate transform and opacity

The lesson on how a browser draws a page showed its steps: style, layout, paint, then compositing the layers together. Animating most properties repeats those steps on every frame. Change `width`, `height`, `top`, or `margin`, and the browser has to lay out the page again, and paint it again, up to 60 times a second. On a phone, it can't keep up, and the animation **janks**: it stutters and skips frames.

`transform` and `opacity` skip layout and paint. The browser can move, scale, rotate, or fade a layer that's already painted, in the compositing step, often on the graphics chip. So:

- To move something, animate `transform: translate(...)`, not `top` or `left`.
- To grow something, animate `transform: scale(...)`, not `width`.
- To fade something, animate `opacity`, not `visibility` or `display`.

`will-change: transform` tells the browser ahead of time that something is about to move, so it can give it a layer of its own before the animation starts. Use it sparingly, on the one or two things that really animate: every layer costs memory, and putting it on everything makes a page slower, not faster.

## Respecting reduced motion

The previous part introduced `prefers-reduced-motion`. The simplest way to respect it is to put movement inside a query that's only true for people who haven't asked for less:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .toast {
    animation: slide-in 200ms ease-out;
  }
}
```

Everyone else still gets the toast, just without the slide. Reduced doesn't have to mean none: a quick fade is usually fine, since it's large movement that causes trouble, not change itself.

## View transitions

A **view transition** animates a whole change on the page, like moving from a list to a detail view, by taking a snapshot of the old view, a snapshot of the new one, and blending between them. By default, it's a cross-fade.

For moving between the pages of one site, all it takes is one rule on both pages:

```css
@view-transition {
  navigation: auto;
}
```

When someone follows a link to another page on the same origin, the old page fades into the new one, instead of flashing white. This works in Chrome, Edge, and Safari, and a browser that doesn't support it, Firefox for now, simply loads the page the usual way, so it's safe to add. A view transition within one page needs one JavaScript call, `document.startViewTransition()`, and that has worked in every current browser since late 2025.

That completes CSS. The next lesson starts on JavaScript, the language that makes a page respond.

examples.code:
.card {
  transition: transform 200ms ease-out, box-shadow 200ms ease-out;
}

.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-4px);
  box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .card {
    transition: box-shadow 200ms ease-out;
  }

  .card:hover {
    transform: none;
  }
}
